<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/deviance-and-crime">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Crime is where sociology's questions sharpen: <strong>what counts as deviance is a social
    decision, enforcement is unevenly distributed, and the response can become part of the
    problem</strong>. The modern field is evidence-first — measuring what actually reduces harm.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="definitions, labelling, mass incarceration, evidence-based prevention" widget="equationStep" config={crimeCfg} />

  <h2>Findings that changed practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Enforcement is distributed by power:</strong> white-collar crime (financial fraud, safety violations) kills and steals multiples of street crime but is polced differently — Sutherland's founding observation, still structurally true.</li>
    <li><strong>Deterrence is narrower than believed:</strong> certainty of being caught deters more than severity of punishment (the evidence is consistent) — knowledge that overturned "tough on crime" assumptions.</li>
    <li><strong>The prosecutor, not the police, holds the machine:</strong> charge decisions drive sentences — a sociological fact invisible at the street level, decisive at the system level.</li>
    <li><strong>Restorative justice tested:</strong> victim–offender dialogue reduces reoffending in trials (mostly property crime) and raises victim satisfaction — one of the field's replicated alternatives to pure punishment.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Durkheim, The Rules of Sociological Method (1895); Becker, Outsiders (1963); National Academies, The Growth of Incarceration (2014); Kennedy, Deterrence and Crime Prevention.</p>
</TopicPage>
